for baba haidara this day should have  come 18 years ago the alleged killer of  his father journalist daeda hydera  finally on trial not in the gambia where  his murder took place but in germany  it's a significant moment not just for a  son but for a country still healing  after more than two decades of often  violent rule under president yahya  jammeh  we  say  this is a day we've been waiting for for  18 years so it's a big day for justice  for my family and myself who started  this journey in 2004.  this is the beginning of a long road  because lowe is just one of the accused  there are others and the most important  one is  who we hope one day to see in front of a  tribunal  bailout is accused of crimes against  humanity and murdering two people at the  request of jamie he allegedly worked as  a driver for the junglers a death squad  that underpinned jammeh's 22-year reign  by silencing his critics and he is the  first alleged member of the gang to go  on trial  we welcome the fact that germany has now  taken the first step  there are other proceedings around the  world there is a procedure in  switzerland and one in the us  in particular we hope that this will  give a boost to justice efforts in the  gambia and that those responsible there  will also be brought to justice  jeremy's regime was notorious for  crushing dissent through murder  disappearances torture rape and  castration his successor adam abaro set  up a south african style truth and  reconciliation commission to probe  allegations of abuses  low is being tried under the principle  of universal jurisdiction allowing a  foreign country to prosecute crimes  against humanity regardless of where  they were committed  analysts say within the gambia there was  not only a lack of judicial resources to  bring about justice but a lack of  political will  so it tells us one thing that you know  perpetrators that are in the gambia as  well as the gambia government is  concerned perfectos that are here will  may probably some of them not all but  some of them will likely escape and  punishment but those that are outside  the gambia and of course other countries  are interested using the principle of  universal jurisdiction  we'll have to deal with them and those  people will have to face justice at the  end of the day  back in the gambia the case is being  watched closely and many hope jammeh  himself will eventually face justice  charlie angela al jazeera
 

